WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.723
One way to let a pipeline into AWS is an access key, saved as a GitHub secret.

2
00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:11.428
That key works until someone deletes it.

3
00:00:11.428 --> 00:00:14.109
So if it leaks, it stays useful for months.

4
00:00:14.933 --> 00:00:17.175
The lab's pipeline stores no key.

5
00:00:17.175 --> 00:00:21.048
It runs when anything under infra changes, in three jobs.

6
00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:25.817
checks runs on every pull request and push, with no cloud login.

7
00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:30.581
Formatting, tflint, a trivy scan, validate, and the module's tests.

8
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.790
The cheap checks run first.

9
00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:37.064
So a formatting slip fails before anything logs in to AWS.

10
00:00:37.767 --> 00:00:41.284
plan runs on pull requests, once the checks pass.

11
00:00:41.284 --> 00:00:48.319
It logs in to AWS, runs terraform plan, and puts the plan on the run's summary page for reviewers.

12
00:00:48.933 --> 00:00:51.093
apply runs after a merge to main.

13
00:00:51.093 --> 00:00:55.935
It waits in the production environment until a required reviewer approves.

14
00:00:55.935 --> 00:00:57.636
Then it plans and applies.

15
00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:00.826
So two jobs log in to AWS.

16
00:01:00.826 --> 00:01:02.433
How, with no key?

17
00:01:03.133 --> 00:01:03.603
OIDC.

18
00:01:03.603 --> 00:01:07.264
First, the job asks GitHub for a token.

19
00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:09.776
GitHub signs a short-lived token.

20
00:01:09.776 --> 00:01:15.096
It holds claims: named fields, such as the repo and branch the job runs for.

21
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.037
The job hands the token to AWS.

22
00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:21.423
But how does AWS know the token is real?

23
00:01:21.423 --> 00:01:22.922
AWS never sees you.

24
00:01:22.922 --> 00:01:25.604
It only trusts GitHub's signature.

25
00:01:25.833 --> 00:01:35.068
An OIDC provider for token.actions.githubusercontent.com lets AWS check that signature.

26
00:01:35.068 --> 00:01:40.588
And the token's aud claim must be sts.amazonaws.com.

27
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.174
Next, the role's trust policy checks the sub claim.

28
00:01:44.174 --> 00:01:47.946
It names your repo and the context, such as pull_request.

29
00:01:47.946 --> 00:01:51.188
It has to match exactly, character for character.

30
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:59.823
If the sub names any other repo, the login fails with: Not authorized to perform sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity.

31
00:01:59.823 --> 00:02:01.568
So check the sub first.

32
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.705
With the right sub, it passes.

33
00:02:03.933 --> 00:02:07.902
Then AWS hands back short-lived credentials for that role.

34
00:02:07.902 --> 00:02:09.749
No key was stored anywhere.

35
00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:13.473
Now terraform plan runs with those credentials.

36
00:02:13.473 --> 00:02:14.927
And the role is small.

37
00:02:14.927 --> 00:02:19.091
It may manage only buckets whose names start with hello-devops.

38
00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:20.777
It cannot change IAM.

39
00:02:20.777 --> 00:02:24.576
So a pull request cannot give the pipeline more power.

40
00:02:24.576 --> 00:02:27.320
You apply IAM changes from your laptop.

41
00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:30.707
The apply job does the same swap after approval.

42
00:02:30.707 --> 00:02:37.253
Its token's sub names the context environment production, and the trust policy must allow that too.

43
00:02:38.767 --> 00:02:40.115
Cheap checks first.

44
00:02:40.115 --> 00:02:43.236
Plan on pull requests, apply after approval.

45
00:02:43.236 --> 00:02:47.634
Each AWS job swaps a signed token for short-lived credentials.

46
00:02:47.634 --> 00:02:51.465
The trust policy checks who, and the role limits what.
